Crucible integration for PyTorch.

Adds DispatchKey::Crucible and the CrucibleState TLS struct that
libcrucible_dispatch.so needs. Verified against pytorch b594e86d432
(2.14.0a0).

WHAT THIS PATCH ADDS A KEY FOR, AND WHY IT DOES NOT RENAME ONE

An earlier revision renamed DeferredInit to Crucible. DeferredInit is
the out-of-core slot torchdistx registers against, and PyTorch's own
FSDP imports torchdistx at torch/distributed/fsdp/_init_utils.py. The
rename removed the "DeferredInit" string from parseDispatchKey, so a
torchdistx build resolving the key by name hit the TORCH_CHECK in
DispatchKey.cpp, and one resolving it by ordinal silently collided with
the Crucible fallback -- Dispatcher::registerFallback rejects a second
fallback on one key. This patch keeps DeferredInit, its name and its
ordinal, and adds Crucible immediately after it.

THE KEY BUDGET IS NOW FULL -- READ BEFORE ADDING ANOTHER KEY

DispatchKeySet is a 64-bit bitset shared between backend and
functionality keys, and c10/core/DispatchKey.h asserts:

    num_backends + num_functionality_keys <= 64

Stock b594e86d432 measures 16 + 47 = 63. This patch takes the last
free slot: 16 + 48 = 64. Verified by compiling the patched header:

    num_functionality_keys = 48
    num_backends           = 16
    SUM                    = 64   HEADROOM = 0
    num_runtime_entries    = 138

A further key makes it 65 and the static_assert fires. Anyone adding
one must first free a slot.

PLACEMENT AND THE PRIORITY IT BUYS

Crucible takes ordinal 41, directly above DeferredInit (40) and
directly below PythonTLSSnapshot (42). Higher enum value means higher
priority -- DispatchKeySet counts leading zeros, so the largest set bit
dispatches first.

Verified by running highestFunctionalityKey() duels against the
patched header:

  Crucible runs BEFORE   DeferredInit, FuncTorchGradWrapper, VmapMode,
                         Batched, FuncTorchBatched, every Autocast*,
                         Tracer, every Autograd* (including the runtime
                         AutogradCPU/AutogradCUDA keys), ADInplaceOrView,
                         ZeroTensor, Functionalize, Python, Fake,
                         BackendSelect, and every backend.

  Crucible runs AFTER    PythonTLSSnapshot, FuncTorchDynamicLayerFrontMode,
                         TESTING_ONLY_GenericWrapper,
                         TESTING_ONLY_GenericMode, PreDispatch,
                         PythonDispatcher.

Crucible is NOT the highest-priority functionality key; PythonDispatcher
(47) is. Being below the functorch front mode, the __torch_dispatch__
entry points and the pre-dispatch tracing keys is intended: those layers
decompose ops and re-enter the dispatcher, so a key above them would
record both an outer op and its decomposition.

Ordinals 42 through 47 each shift up by one. Every in-tree user is
recompiled with the fork. An out-of-core extension built against stock
PyTorch must be rebuilt, as it must be for any forked c10 anyway --
DeferredInit is the one such key kept ordinal-stable on purpose.

Apply with:  git apply patches/pytorch-crucible-integration.patch

diff --git a/c10/core/CrucibleState.h b/c10/core/CrucibleState.h
new file mode 100644
index 00000000000..23d27c1939a
--- /dev/null
+++ b/c10/core/CrucibleState.h
@@ -0,0 +1,104 @@
+#pragma once
+
+#include <c10/macros/Export.h>
+#include <cstdint>
+
+namespace c10 {
+
+// Runtime mode for the Crucible execute-and-record system.
+//
+//   INACTIVE  — fallback is a no-op passthrough (dispatch key may be set
+//               but mode says do nothing; fast path)
+//   RECORD   — ops execute eagerly AND are recorded into the ring buffer
+//   COMPILED — compiled trace replays, guards checked per-op
+//   DIVERGED — guard failed mid-trace; transitional state that reverts
+//              to RECORD so the diverging op is properly recorded
+//
+// State machine:
+//   INACTIVE ⇄ RECORD → COMPILED ↔ DIVERGED → RECORD
+//
+enum class CrucibleMode : uint8_t {
+  INACTIVE = 0,
+  RECORD = 1,
+  COMPILED = 2,
+  DIVERGED = 3,
+};
+
+// Thread-local state for Crucible. The dispatch key (DispatchKey::Crucible)
+// gates WHETHER the fallback fires. This struct tells it WHAT to do.
+//
+// Pattern matches AutogradState (c10/core/AutogradState.h):
+// a plain struct with static TLS accessors.
+//
+// context_ is opaque (void*) because c10 cannot depend on
+// torch/csrc/crucible/ headers. The actual CrucibleContext lives in
+// torch/csrc/crucible/CrucibleContext.h and is static_cast'd there.
+// This is the same layering strategy as AutogradMeta (c10 declares,
+// torch defines the concrete type).
+struct C10_API CrucibleState {
+  [[nodiscard]] static CrucibleState& get_tls_state();
+  static void set_tls_state(CrucibleState state);
+
+  [[nodiscard]] CrucibleMode mode() const {
+    return mode_;
+  }
+  void set_mode(CrucibleMode m) {
+    mode_ = m;
+  }
+
+  // Opaque pointer to the per-thread CrucibleContext (torch level).
+  // nullptr when Crucible is not active on this thread.
+  [[nodiscard]] void* context() const {
+    return context_;
+  }
+  void set_context(void* ctx) {
+    context_ = ctx;
+  }
+
+  // Op index within the current trace (COMPILED mode guard checking).
+  [[nodiscard]] uint32_t op_index() const {
+    return op_index_;
+  }
+  void set_op_index(uint32_t idx) {
+    op_index_ = idx;
+  }
+  // Advance and return the PREVIOUS index (post-increment).
+  [[nodiscard]] uint32_t advance_op_index() {
+    return op_index_++;
+  }
+
+  // Scope hash: FNV-1a of the current nn.Module path (e.g. "layer1.conv1").
+  // Set by Python forward_pre_hooks via crucible_dispatch_set_tls_scope(),
+  // read by the C++ fallback kernel per-op. Zero when no module scope active.
+  [[nodiscard]] uint64_t scope_hash() const {
+    return scope_hash_;
+  }
+  void set_scope_hash(uint64_t h) {
+    scope_hash_ = h;
+  }
+
+ private:
+  CrucibleMode mode_ = CrucibleMode::INACTIVE;
+  void* context_ = nullptr;
+  uint32_t op_index_ = 0;
+  uint64_t scope_hash_ = 0;
+};
+
+// RAII guard that saves/restores the full CrucibleState on the
+// current thread. Non-copyable and non-movable — moving would leave
+// prev_ in an indeterminate state, and the destructor would restore
+// garbage into TLS.
+struct C10_API CrucibleModeGuard {
+  explicit CrucibleModeGuard(CrucibleMode mode);
+  ~CrucibleModeGuard();
+
+  CrucibleModeGuard(const CrucibleModeGuard&) = delete;
+  CrucibleModeGuard& operator=(const CrucibleModeGuard&) = delete;
+  CrucibleModeGuard(CrucibleModeGuard&&) = delete;
+  CrucibleModeGuard& operator=(CrucibleModeGuard&&) = delete;
+
+ private:
+  CrucibleState prev_;
+};
+
+} // namespace c10
diff --git a/c10/core/CrucibleState.cpp b/c10/core/CrucibleState.cpp
new file mode 100644
index 00000000000..98b176681ac
--- /dev/null
+++ b/c10/core/CrucibleState.cpp
@@ -0,0 +1,26 @@
+#include <c10/core/CrucibleState.h>
+
+namespace c10 {
+
+namespace {
+thread_local CrucibleState crucible_state_tls;
+} // namespace
+
+CrucibleState& CrucibleState::get_tls_state() {
+  return crucible_state_tls;
+}
+
+void CrucibleState::set_tls_state(CrucibleState state) {
+  crucible_state_tls = state;
+}
+
+CrucibleModeGuard::CrucibleModeGuard(CrucibleMode mode)
+    : prev_(CrucibleState::get_tls_state()) {
+  CrucibleState::get_tls_state().set_mode(mode);
+}
+
+CrucibleModeGuard::~CrucibleModeGuard() {
+  CrucibleState::set_tls_state(prev_);
+}
+
+} // namespace c10
