   // See https://pytorch.org/torchdistx/latest/deferred_init.html
   DeferredInit,
 
+  // Crucible: execute-and-record compilation runtime.
+  //
+  // Placement is deliberate, and the position is the contract. A boxed
+  // fallback here runs BEFORE, and therefore sees:
+  //   - every backend (Dense, Sparse, Quantized, NestedTensor, ...)
+  //   - BackendSelect, Fake, Python, Functionalize
+  //   - Conjugate, Negative, ZeroTensor, ADInplaceOrView
+  //   - every Autograd* key, so autograd-decomposed backward ops are seen
+  //   - Tracer and every Autocast* key, so the post-cast op is recorded
+  //   - FuncTorchBatched, Batched, VmapMode, FuncTorchGradWrapper
+  //
+  // It runs AFTER, and therefore does NOT see ahead of:
+  //   - PythonTLSSnapshot
+  //   - FuncTorchDynamicLayerFrontMode
+  //   - TESTING_ONLY_GenericWrapper, TESTING_ONLY_GenericMode
+  //   - PreDispatch
+  //   - PythonDispatcher
+  //
+  // That is the intended order, not a limitation. Sitting below the
+  // functorch front mode, the __torch_dispatch__ entry points and the
+  // pre-dispatch tracing keys means Crucible records the ops that
+  // actually execute, once each. A key above them would also see each
+  // op those layers decompose, and record both the outer op and its
+  // decomposition.
+  //
+  // Higher enum value means higher priority: DispatchKeySet counts
+  // leading zeros to pick the winner, so the largest set bit dispatches
+  // first. Moving this enumerator changes what the fallback intercepts.
+  Crucible,
+
   // Used by Python key logic to know the set of tls on entry to the dispatcher
   // This kernel assumes it is the top-most non-functorch-related DispatchKey.
   // If you add a key above, make sure to update the fallback implementation for
